  "updated": "2026-11-25T15:39:39.557700+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\n\nStrengthening high pressure exiting the northern Great Lakes and \nbuilding into southern Quebec (~1045 mb) will provide one more dry \nday today, though easterly winds will be on the increase. Local \nprobabilistic guidance suggests gusts of 25 to 30 mph in the \nafternoon, with daytime highs remaining near seasonal norms in the \nlow to mid 40s.\n\nA strong upper-level wave and PV anomaly descending south through \nthe eastern Plains will merge with the upper-level wave over the \nwestern Gulf Coast states, leading to an anomalously deep upper-\nlevel trough over the Gulf and Gulf Coast states as the trough axis \nbecomes negatively tilted. Differential cyclonic vorticity advection \n(DCVA) and strong diffluence aloft ahead of the negatively tilted \naxis will support robust surface cyclogenesis and a tightening \nbaroclinic zone oriented roughly southwest-to-northeast across the \nOhio Valley and lower Great Lakes. An anomalously strong meridional \nmoisture transport plume develops in response to the amplified ridge-\ntrough couplet. Deep tropical and subtropical moisture sourced from \nboth the western Atlantic and the Caribbean Sea is drawn \nnorthwestward into the system's warm conveyor belt (WCB). Elevated \nequivalent potential temperature (theta-e) air and precipitable \nwater (PWAT) values surging well above seasonal climatology will \nride up the sloped frontal and isentropic surfaces over Southeast \nMichigan. Strong low-level jet (LLJ) convergence and deep isentropic \nascent favor a broad shield of rain overspreading the CWA by \nThursday afternoon into Thursday night, with periods of moderate to \npotentially locally heavy rain where frontogenetic banding and WCB \nmoisture flux maximize. Total rainfall looks to approach 1 inch \nacross most locations in Southeast Michigan. Euro ensembles even \nshowing a 70 percent chance of 24-hour precipitation amounts \nreaching at or above 1 inch across the eastern third of the CWA. \nThis rainfall will wipe out much of the extreme rainfall deficit for \nthe month of November, as rainfall totals thus far are only 0.18 \ninches at Detroit and 0.33 inches at Flint, far below the normal \nvalue near 2 inches.\n\nThermal profiles on Thursday are comfortably warm enough to keep \nprecipitation all rain, with daytime highs in the low to mid 40s.\nAs the surface low deepens and tracks northeastward toward the \neastern Great Lakes and Saint Lawrence Valley by late Thursday night \ninto Friday, wrap-around cold advection on the back side of the \nsystem will cool the column. A transition or brief mix with wet snow \nremains possible early Friday morning, though marginal surface and \nboundary-layer temperatures alongside wet ground conditions should \nlimit any accumulation if a changeover even occurs, which looks \ndoubtful as surface dewpoints on Friday look to remain in the mid to \nupper 30s.\n\nGusty winds are also expected Thursday into Friday as the pressure \ngradient tightens around the departing cyclone. The strength of the \nlow-level jet and pressure gradient on Thursday suggests easterly \nwind gusts reaching around 35 mph, while northwest winds on the back \nside Friday should come in a bit lower due to the lack of strong \ncold advection.\n\nBy Saturday, the extreme amplification relaxes as the upper-level \ntrough deamplifies and translates eastward, leaving a cooler, more \nzonal to weakly cyclonic flow regime across the Great Lakes. Renewed \namplification/merger with a strong upper level wave coming out of \nthe Rockies will bring another good shot of appreciable rainfall by \nthe end of the weekend, as low pressure tracks through the Eastern \nOhio Valley. A changeover to snow at the tail end Sunday night is \nnot out of the question with the favorable night timing and slightly \ncolder thermal profiles compared to the Thanksgiving storm.",
